From d8567f37a4f95996554cf99d95b5f713b66d2caa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 09:21:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 119/119] fix: the glare source budget's own top end could hang
 the editor, so it now has a ceiling

r_HDRDiffractionMaxSources was introduced with 0 = unlimited as the escape hatch. The escape hatch
was the hazard. Swept on the airfield with the sun in frame and Streak Amount at 200: below 256 the
sun's star FLICKERS, 256 is the first value at which it holds still, 512 fans it up, 1024 costs a
large slice of the frame rate, 2048 is the LAST value at which the picture changes at all, 16384
puts a star on every reflection at a few fps, and 32768 ran at 2 fps and held the main thread long
enough for Windows to kill Sandbox as AppHangB1. At the shipping default Streak Amount 50 in
daylight, 10 and 100 are indistinguishable. The useful range is entirely inside [256, 2048] and
everything above it is paid for and not seen.

So: a hard cap of 4096 - one stop of headroom over the last setting that alters the picture -
clamped in ExecuteDiffraction at the CONSUMER rather than as a REGISTER_CVAR3 range, because a
value set in a config or before this DLL loaded would otherwise walk straight past a registration
range and the number that has to be safe is the one the pass actually draws with. 0 or less no
longer means unlimited; it means the cap. The default stays 256. A clamped value is printed in the
debug report rather than swallowed - silently ignoring a console command is worse than refusing it.

r_HDRDiffractionDebug 3's once-a-second report also gains the COST next to the count:

  [BLOOM]            GPU 1.83 ms in the sprite pass + 0.11 ms in the composite = 1.94 ms of this
                     frame, for 261 sprites (7.4 us each)

No new instrumentation was needed. The pass already carries PROFILE_LABEL_SCOPE markers
(HDR_DIFFRACTION, with the budget chain nested inside it, and HDR_DIFFRACTION_COMPOSITE), and
CRenderPipelineProfiler times every labelled section with GPU timestamps and publishes them as
detailed stats keyed by that label, so the report looks the two up by name off the render thread's
array. Smoothed rather than instantaneous: a once-a-second line built from one frame's timestamps
jitters by more than the difference being read. It is sampled at PRINT time, not captured with the
rest of the report, because the profiler's sections resolve two or three frames behind the draw.

What it deliberately does not do is switch the profiler on. Recording costs a timestamp pair per
section for the whole frame and is governed by r_profiler / r_DisplayInfo 3, which are engine-wide
switches and not this debug mode's to flip; when it is off the line says so instead of printing a
zero that would read as "free".

Recorded in decisions/s8-glare-budget.md as a dated addendum, with the sweep table and the
observation that explains the flicker below 256: the energy cutoff selects TEXELS, and the sun's
disc is not one source but hundreds of half-res texels within a stop of each other, so the cutoff
lands inside a single physical object and the controller moves it every frame - which half of the
disc survives changes frame to frame. Approach B (source-space blob merge, sprite at the block's
energy-weighted centroid) is promoted from "kept" to the next step: the measurement answers all
three of the grounds it was rejected on, and no choice of N removes a within-blob artefact.

Docs: DiffractionKernelSpec 3.1 gains the sweep table, the cap and the cost line; ConsoleReference
gains a Hard cap row and the profiler caveat under Measuring.

